// Question library: puzzles from BrainStellar's collection (MIT licence), rewritten in our own
// words with our own hints and worked solutions. Puzzles Theo already had (the two-bullet revolver,
// the five pirates, Monty Hall, the 100 doors, a fair toss from a biased coin, the two-egg drop and
// others) are left out. Answers are re-derived in research/verify_library.py.
(function () {
  const EXACT = { abs: 0.5, rel: 0 };

  QT.library.add('bs', [
    ['explorer', 'Logic', 'e', 'The returning explorer',
      `An explorer walks 1 mile south, then 1 mile east, then 1 mile north, and ends up exactly where they started.`,
      { open: `<p><b>The North Pole</b> works: south and north are the same line of longitude, and walking east just circles the pole at a fixed distance. So the bear is a polar bear: white.</p><p>There are also infinitely many starting points near the <b>South Pole</b>: start 1 mile north of a circle of latitude whose circumference is 1/n miles. The southward mile takes you onto that circle, the eastward mile goes round it n times, and the northward mile brings you back. (No bears there, though.)</p>`,
        h: [`East and west movement is along a circle of latitude. Where on Earth does such a circle shrink to almost nothing?`, `At one special point, "south" from it and "north" back to it are the same line, whatever you do in between.`, `Ask whether that's the only place: could walking east bring you back to the same longitude after going round a small circle?`] }],

    ['switch', 'Logic', 'e', 'Which switch?',
      `Three switches outside a closed room control one light bulb inside; only one of them works. All start off. You can flip switches as often as you like, but once you open the door you can't touch them again.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest times you need to enter the room to know which switch controls the bulb?`, a: 1, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`On or off only gives you two outcomes. You need to tell three switches apart.`, `A bulb gives off more than light when it's been on for a while.`, `Leave one switch on long enough to warm the bulb, then change things before you go in.`],
        s: [`Use a second property of the bulb (heat) to get three distinguishable outcomes from one visit.`,
          [`Turn switch 1 on for ten minutes, then off. Turn switch 2 on and go in.`, `Bulb on: switch 2. Bulb off but warm: switch 1. Bulb off and cold: switch 3.`, `So <b>1</b> visit is enough.`],
          `One visit with three distinct outcomes covers three switches exactly.`, `Answering 2 by treating the bulb as only on/off.`] }]],

    ['candy', 'Probability', 'e', 'Lucky candy',
      `You have 50 good and 50 rotten sweets and two boxes. You may split them between the boxes however you like (neither box empty). Then a box is chosen at random and a sweet drawn from it at random.`,
      [{ q: `What is the highest possible probability of drawing a good sweet?`, a: 74 / 99,
        h: [`The chance of each box is 1/2 whatever it holds, so a box with very few sweets can be very reliable.`, `Try putting a single good sweet in one box on its own.`, `Then P(good) = ½ × 1 + ½ × P(good from the other box).`],
        s: [`Make one box a guaranteed win with as few good sweets as possible, and leave the rest in the other.`,
          [`Box 1: one good sweet. Box 2: 49 good and 50 rotten.`, `P = ½ × 1 + ½ × 49/99.`, `= 148/198 = <b>74/99 ≈ 0.747</b>.`],
          `It beats the 0.5 from splitting evenly, and it can't reach 1 because box 2 must hold the rotten sweets.`, `Splitting the sweets evenly between the boxes, which gives exactly 0.5.`] }]],

    ['girls', 'Expected value', 'e', 'A world that wants girls',
      `Every family keeps having children until they have a girl, then stops. Each birth is a boy or a girl with probability ½, independently.`,
      [{ q: `(a) In the long run, what fraction of all children born are boys?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`Look at a single birth, whichever family it happens in.`, `Does a family's stopping rule change the chance that the next baby is a boy?`, `Every birth is a fair coin toss; stopping rules decide how many tosses happen, not how they land.`],
        s: [`Stopping rules can't bias fair, independent births.`,
          [`Each birth is a boy with probability ½, whatever the family's history.`, `So the expected numbers of boys and girls born are equal: the fraction of boys is <b>½</b>.`],
          `Each family has exactly one girl and, on average, one boy (part b), so 1 : 1.`, `Thinking families "stop at a girl" so there will be more girls.`] },
      { q: `(b) On average, how many children does a family have?`, a: 2,
        h: [`The number of children is the number of tosses up to and including the first girl.`, `That's a geometric distribution with success probability ½.`, `A geometric distribution with success probability p has mean 1/p.`],
        s: [`Births until the first girl are geometric with p = ½.`,
          [`E[children] = 1/p = <b>2</b>: one girl and, on average, one boy.`],
          `Matches part (a): one boy for every girl.`, ``] }]],

    ['tigers', 'Game theory', 'e', 'Tigers and the sheep',
      `100 tigers and one sheep live on an island with plenty of grass. A tiger that eats the sheep turns into a sheep itself, and can then be eaten. Tigers would like to eat the sheep, but above all want to survive. All are perfectly rational.`,
      { open: `<p><b>The sheep survives.</b> Work up from small cases. With 1 tiger, it eats the sheep (no one can eat it afterwards). With 2 tigers, neither eats: whoever did would become a sheep facing 1 tiger and be eaten. With 3, a tiger can eat safely, because it leaves the 2-tiger case where the new sheep survives. In general the sheep is eaten when the number of tigers is odd and survives when it's even. 100 is even, so no tiger moves.</p>`,
        h: [`Start with 1 tiger, then 2, then 3. What happens in each case?`, `A tiger that eats becomes the sheep in the game with one fewer tiger.`, `So eating is safe exactly when the sheep would survive in the smaller game. Look for the pattern by parity.`] }],

    ['duck-fox', 'Game theory', 'm', 'Duck and fox',
      `A duck is in the middle of a circular pond of radius R. A fox that can't swim runs round the edge four times as fast as the duck swims. The duck can only take off from the shore.`,
      [{ q: `(a) The duck swims in circles round the centre. What is the largest radius, as a fraction of R, at which it can still circle faster (in angle) than the fox?`, a: 0.25,
        h: [`Compare angular speeds, not distances: the duck at radius r and the fox at radius R.`, `Angular speed is speed divided by radius.`, `Set v/r equal to 4v/R and solve for r.`],
        s: [`The duck wins the angular race wherever v/r > 4v/R.`,
          [`v/r > 4v/R ⇔ r < R/4.`, `So just inside radius <b>R/4 = 0.25R</b> the duck can gain angle on the fox until it is directly opposite.`],
          `At r = R/4 their angular speeds are equal, so it has to be strictly inside.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) From just inside that circle, directly opposite the fox, the duck swims straight for the shore. Up to what speed ratio (fox speed ÷ duck speed) does this escape work?`, a: 1 + Math.PI, tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Let the fox be k times as fast. The duck can get opposite the fox on a circle of radius R/k.`, `It then swims R − R/k to the shore while the fox runs half the circumference, πR.`, `The duck escapes if its swim takes less time than the fox's run. Solve for k.`],
        s: [`Race the duck's straight swim against the fox's half lap.`,
          [`With fox speed kv, the duck can circle opposite at radius R/k, then swim (1 − 1/k)R.`, `Duck's time (1 − 1/k)R/v; fox's time πR/(kv).`, `Duck wins if k − 1 < π, i.e. k < <b>1 + π ≈ 4.14</b>.`],
          `k = 4 is below 4.14, so a duck facing a 4× fox escapes.`, `Swimming straight out from the centre: the duck needs R/v while the fox needs πR/(4v), and π/4 < 1.`] }]],

    ['plane-wind', 'Logic', 'e', 'The plane in the wind',
      `A plane flies 1,000 km from A to B and straight back at an airspeed of 500 km/h. A steady 100 km/h wind blows from A towards B for the whole trip.`,
      [{ q: `How many hours does the round trip take? (With no wind it would take 4.)`, a: 25 / 6,
        h: [`With the wind behind it the plane's ground speed is airspeed + wind; against it, airspeed − wind.`, `Find each leg's time separately and add.`, `The slow leg lasts longer than the fast leg, so the gain and the loss don't cancel.`],
        s: [`Time on each leg is distance ÷ ground speed; the slow leg dominates.`,
          [`A to B: 1000/600 = 5/3 h. B to A: 1000/400 = 5/2 h.`, `Total = 5/3 + 5/2 = <b>25/6 ≈ 4.17 h</b>.`],
          `In general the time grows by the factor 1/(1 − (w/s)²) = 1/0.96 ≈ 1.042.`, `Assuming the speed-up and slow-down cancel, giving 4 hours.`] }]],

    ['cords', 'Logic', 'e', 'Burning cords',
      `You have two cords. Each takes exactly an hour to burn from one end, but they burn unevenly, so half a cord's length doesn't mean half an hour.`,
      [{ q: `(a) You light cord 1 at one end and cord 2 at both ends at the same moment. When cord 2 has burnt out, how many minutes of burning are left in cord 1?`, a: 30, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Lit at both ends, a cord's two flames together use up an hour's worth of burning.`, `So cord 2 burns out after half the time it would take from one end.`, `Cord 1 has been burning from one end for that long.`],
        s: [`Two flames burn an hour's worth of cord in half the time, however unevenly it burns.`,
          [`Cord 2 burns out after 30 minutes.`, `Cord 1 has burned for 30 minutes from one end, so <b>30</b> minutes of burning remain.`],
          `Each cord holds 60 minutes of burning in total, whatever its shape.`, `Measuring by length, which fails because the burn rate varies.`] },
      { q: `(b) At that moment you light the other end of cord 1. How many minutes until it burns out?`, a: 15, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Cord 1 now has some minutes of one-end burning left, and you've added a second flame.`, `Two flames use up the remaining burn time twice as fast.`, `Halve your answer to (a).`],
        s: [`Lighting the second end halves whatever burning time remains.`,
          [`30 minutes of one-end burning, now consumed from both ends: <b>15</b> minutes.`],
          `Total from the start: 30 + 15 = 45 minutes, the other classic interval these cords can time.`, ``] }]],

    ['socks-pigeonhole', 'Combinatorics', 'e', 'Pairs of socks',
      `A drawer holds 10 black and 10 red socks. You take socks out in the dark.`,
      [{ q: `(a) How many must you take to be sure of a matching pair?`, a: 3, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Think about the worst luck you could have.`, `With two colours, how many socks could you hold without a pair?`, `One more than that forces a match (the pigeonhole principle).`],
        s: [`Pigeonhole: more socks than colours forces two of a colour.`,
          [`Two socks can be one of each colour.`, `The third must match one of them: <b>3</b>.`],
          `With n colours you'd need n + 1.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) How many must you take to be sure of a red pair?`, a: 12, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Now the worst case is drawing the colour you don't want.`, `How many black socks could you draw before any red ones?`, `After all the black socks, you need two reds.`],
        s: [`The worst case uses up every sock of the wrong colour first.`,
          [`You could draw all 10 black socks.`, `Then two more are both red: 10 + 2 = <b>12</b>.`],
          `Check the case of 11: ten black and one red, no red pair.`, `Answering 3 again: that guarantees some pair, not a red one.`] }]],

    ['antipodal', 'Logic', 'm', 'Opposite points',
      `Temperature varies continuously over the Earth's surface.`,
      { open: `<p>Take any great circle, such as the equator, and let t(θ) be the temperature at angle θ. Define f(θ) = t(θ) − t(θ + π), the difference between a point and its opposite.</p><p>Then f(π) = t(π) − t(2π) = t(π) − t(0) = −f(0). So either f(0) = 0 already, or f takes opposite signs at 0 and π. f is continuous, so by the intermediate value theorem it is zero somewhere in between. At that θ the two opposite points have <b>exactly the same temperature</b>.</p>`,
        h: [`Compare each point with the point directly opposite it: look at the difference in temperature.`, `Go halfway round a great circle. What happens to that difference?`, `A continuous function that changes sign must cross zero (the intermediate value theorem).`] }],

    ['half-hour', 'Probability', 'e', 'Accidents in half an hour',
      `On a certain road the probability of at least one accident in an hour is 3/4. Assume accidents in separate stretches of time are independent and the rate is constant.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability of at least one accident in half an hour?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`Work with "no accident", which multiplies across independent periods.`, `An hour with no accident is two half-hours with no accident.`, `If q is P(no accident in half an hour), then q² = 1 − 3/4.`],
        s: [`Probabilities of "nothing happens" multiply over independent intervals.`,
          [`P(no accident in an hour) = 1/4 = q².`, `q = 1/2, so P(at least one in half an hour) = 1 − 1/2 = <b>1/2</b>.`],
          `Two half-hours: 1 − (1/2)² = 3/4, as given.`, `Halving 3/4 to get 3/8: probabilities of "at least one" don't scale linearly.`] }]],

    ['hats-line', 'Logic', 'm', 'Prisoners in hats',
      `100 prisoners stand in a line, each wearing a red or blue hat. Each sees every hat in front of them but not their own or those behind. Starting from the back, each says "red" or "blue" aloud, and goes free if it matches their own hat. Everyone hears every answer. They can agree a plan beforehand.`,
      [{ q: `How many prisoners can the best plan guarantee to free?`, a: 99, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`The prisoner at the back can't know their own hat, but they can pass on information.`, `One word can carry one bit: say, whether the number of red hats they see is even or odd.`, `Each later prisoner knows that parity, sees the hats in front, and has heard everyone behind them.`],
        s: [`The last prisoner sacrifices their guess to announce a parity; everyone else deduces their own hat.`,
          [`The back prisoner says "red" if they see an even number of red hats, "blue" if odd.`, `Each prisoner after that counts the red hats ahead plus the red answers already given (all correct), and compares with the announced parity to find their own colour.`, `So <b>99</b> are guaranteed, and the first has a 50% chance.`],
          `No plan can guarantee the first prisoner, who has no information about their own hat.`, `Pairing up prisoners, which only guarantees about half.`] }]],

    ['knight-knave', 'Logic', 'e', 'Knight and knave',
      `At a fork in the road stand two brothers. One always tells the truth and one always lies; you don't know which is which. One road is right.`,
      { open: `<p>Ask either brother: <b>"If I asked your brother which road is right, which would he point to?"</b> Then take the other road.</p><p>If you asked the truth-teller, he truthfully reports the liar's wrong answer. If you asked the liar, he lies about the truth-teller's right answer. Either way you hear the wrong road. (A question that routes through both of them always comes out as one lie.)</p>`,
        h: [`One direct question gets a true or false answer depending on who you ask. You need an answer that doesn't depend on that.`, `Make the answer pass through both brothers, so it always contains exactly one lie.`, `Ask what the other brother would say.`] }],

    ['water-wine', 'Logic', 'e', 'Water and wine',
      `One glass holds 100 ml of water and another 100 ml of wine. You move 10 ml of water into the wine and stir, then move 10 ml of the mixture back into the water.`,
      [{ q: `How many ml of wine are now in the water glass?`, a: 100 / 11,
        h: [`After the first pour the wine glass holds 110 ml, of which 100 ml is wine.`, `10 ml of that mixture carries wine in proportion 100/110.`, `Shortcut: both glasses end with 100 ml, so any wine missing from the wine glass has been replaced by the same volume of water.`],
        s: [`Track the wine in the 10 ml taken back.`,
          [`The wine glass has 100 ml wine in 110 ml.`, `10 ml of it holds 10 × 100/110 = <b>100/11 ≈ 9.09 ml</b> of wine.`],
          `The water in the wine glass is 10 − 10 × 10/110 = 100/11 too: they are always equal.`, `Thinking the water glass ends up "less contaminated" because the mixture was diluted.`] }]],

    ['trains', 'Probability', 'e', 'Waiting for the train',
      `Trains to the north and to the south each run every 10 minutes, but each southbound train leaves 1 minute after a northbound one. A commuter arrives at a random moment and takes whichever train comes first.`,
      [{ q: `What fraction of the time does the commuter go south?`, a: 0.1,
        h: [`Draw a 10-minute cycle with the two departure times on it.`, `The southbound train comes first only if you arrive in the gap just before it.`, `How long is that gap, compared with the whole cycle?`],
        s: [`The first train is the one whose departure you arrive just before.`,
          [`Southbound comes first only if you arrive in the 1 minute between a northbound and the next southbound.`, `So P(south) = 1/10 = <b>0.1</b>.`],
          `Both lines run equally often, yet one is taken 9 times as often: it's the timing, not the frequency.`, `Answering ½ because both trains run equally often.`] }]],

    ['e-pi', 'Logic', 'm', 'e to the π or π to the e',
      `Without a calculator, decide which is larger: e<sup>π</sup> or π<sup>e</sup>.`,
      { open: `<p><b>e<sup>π</sup> is larger.</b> Take the (πe)-th root of both: compare e<sup>1/e</sup> with π<sup>1/π</sup>. The function x<sup>1/x</sup> = e<sup>(ln x)/x</sup> has derivative proportional to (1 − ln x)/x², so it rises up to x = e and falls after. Its maximum is at x = e, so e<sup>1/e</sup> > π<sup>1/π</sup>, and raising both sides to the power πe gives e<sup>π</sup> > π<sup>e</sup>.</p><p>(Numerically 23.14 against 22.46.)</p>`,
        h: [`Both numbers have the form a<sup>b</sup>. Take roots so each side depends on one number only.`, `Compare e<sup>1/e</sup> with π<sup>1/π</sup>.`, `Where is x<sup>1/x</sup> largest? Differentiate (ln x)/x.`] }],

    ['josephus-2', 'Logic', 'm', 'Shooting round the circle',
      `People stand in a circle numbered 1, 2, 3, …. Person 1 eliminates person 2, person 3 eliminates person 4, and so on round and round, each survivor eliminating the next person still standing, until one is left.`,
      [{ q: `(a) With 1,024 people, which position survives?`, a: 1, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Try small powers of two: 2, 4, 8 people.`, `Each full lap halves the circle, and when the count is a power of two the lap ends with person 1 about to shoot again.`, `So with a power of two, nothing changes who starts each lap.`],
        s: [`With 2<sup>k</sup> people every lap halves the circle and person 1 starts the next lap.`,
          [`1,024 = 2<sup>10</sup>: after each lap the count is still a power of two and person 1 shoots first.`, `So position <b>1</b> survives.`],
          `Check with 4 people: 1 shoots 2, 3 shoots 4, 1 shoots 3.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) With 100 people?`, a: 73, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Remove people until the number left is a power of two; whoever is about to shoot then wins.`, `The largest power of two below 100 is 64, so 36 people must go first.`, `After 36 eliminations, it is the turn of the person just after the 36th one eliminated.`],
        s: [`Write n = 2<sup>k</sup> + m; the survivor is 2m + 1.`,
          [`100 = 64 + 36.`, `After 36 eliminations (persons 2, 4, …, 72), person 73 is about to shoot with 64 left.`, `So position <b>73</b> survives.`],
          `The formula gives 1 for powers of two, matching part (a).`, ``] }]],

    ['invisible-die', 'Probability', 'm', 'The invisible die',
      `A normal die is rolled together with a second, unseen six-sided die. Every total from 1 to 12 comes up with probability 1/12.`,
      [{ q: `How many faces of the unseen die show 0?`, a: 3, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Totals of 1 to 6 must come from the normal die plus something small; totals of 7 to 12 from something bigger.`, `If the unseen die shows only two values, a and b, the totals are uniform on two blocks of six.`, `Try a = 0 and b = 6. How often must each appear?`],
        s: [`The totals split into 1–6 and 7–12, so the unseen die adds 0 or 6, each half the time.`,
          [`Faces 0, 0, 0, 6, 6, 6: totals 1–6 each with probability ½ × 1/6 = 1/12, and 7–12 likewise.`, `So <b>3</b> faces show 0.`],
          `Twelve totals, each 1/12, add up to 1.`, ``] }]],

    ['dark-deck', 'Logic', 'm', 'Cards in a dark room',
      `In a pitch-dark room you're handed a 52-card deck in which exactly 10 cards are face up, the rest face down. You can't see or feel which are which.`,
      { open: `<p>Count off <b>any 10 cards</b> into a second pile and <b>turn that whole pile over</b>.</p><p>Say the 10 cards you took include k face-up cards. Then the big pile has 10 − k face-up cards left. Your small pile had k face up and 10 − k face down; turned over, it has 10 − k face up. Both piles now have 10 − k face-up cards, whatever k was.</p>`,
        h: [`You can't tell which cards are face up, so the method must work whatever you happen to pick.`, `Take a pile of the right size at random. Call the number of face-up cards in it k.`, `What happens to the face-up count of that pile if you flip the whole pile over?`] }],

    ['cheating', 'Logic', 'h', 'The unfaithful husbands',
      `In a town of 100 couples, every wife knows which other husbands are unfaithful but not whether her own is. A wife who becomes certain her husband is unfaithful throws him out that midnight, and everyone sees. Everyone is a perfect reasoner. One day the mayor announces that at least one husband is unfaithful. Nothing happens for nine nights; on the tenth night some husbands are thrown out.`,
      [{ q: `How many husbands are thrown out on the tenth night?`, a: 10, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Start with one unfaithful husband. His wife sees no others, so what does she conclude from the announcement?`, `With two, each of their wives sees one. When nothing happens on night 1, what does each learn?`, `Build it up: with k unfaithful husbands, their wives act on night k.`],
        s: [`Induction on the number k of unfaithful husbands: their wives all act on night k.`,
          [`k = 1: his wife sees no one else, so it must be hers; she acts on night 1.`, `k = 2: each wife sees one. When night 1 passes quietly, she knows it isn't the one-husband case, so her own is the second; both act on night 2.`, `In general, k quiet nights rule out "k or fewer". Action on night 10 means <b>10</b> husbands.`],
          `The announcement matters even though every wife already knew there was at least one: it makes it common knowledge.`, ``] }]],

    ['father-lies', 'Conditional probability', 'e', 'The father who lies',
      `A father says it snowed last night. It snows on 1 night in 8, and he lies 5 times out of 6.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability it actually snowed?`, a: 1 / 36,
        h: [`Use Bayes: weigh "it snowed and he told the truth" against "it didn't and he lied".`, `P(says snow and it snowed) = 1/8 × 1/6.`, `P(says snow and it didn't) = 7/8 × 5/6. Divide the first by the sum.`],
        s: [`Bayes: compare the two ways he could come to say it snowed.`,
          [`True claim: 1/8 × 1/6 = 1/48. False claim: 7/8 × 5/6 = 35/48.`, `P(snow | he says so) = 1/(1 + 35) = <b>1/36</b>.`],
          `A habitual liar's "yes" is evidence against snow: 1/36 is below the 1/8 base rate.`, `Answering 1/6, his chance of telling the truth, and ignoring how rare snow is.`] }]],

    ['devil-penny', 'Game theory', 'm', "The devil's penny",
      `You and the devil take turns putting identical coins flat on a round table, never overlapping. Whoever can't place a coin loses. You may go first.`,
      { open: `<p>Put your first coin <b>exactly in the centre</b>. After that, whenever the devil places a coin, put yours at the point <b>diametrically opposite</b> it through the centre.</p><p>The position is symmetric after each of your moves, so if the devil found room, the mirror-image spot is free too (it can't overlap his new coin, since only the centre is its own mirror image, and that's taken). So you always have a move, and the devil runs out first.</p>`,
        h: [`Look for a way to answer any move the devil makes.`, `The table is symmetric about its centre. Could you copy his moves?`, `Copying only works if the centre, the one point that is its own mirror image, is already taken.`] }],

    ['poison-wine', 'Combinatorics', 'm', 'The poisoned bottle',
      `One of 1,000 bottles of wine is poisoned. Poison takes three weeks to act and you have four, so you get a single round of tests: each tester can sip from any number of bottles, and after three weeks you see who has died.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest testers that always identifies the poisoned bottle?`, a: 10, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Each tester ends up in one of two states, so n testers give 2<sup>n</sup> possible outcomes.`, `You need at least as many outcomes as bottles.`, `Number the bottles in binary and let tester i drink from every bottle whose i-th bit is 1.`],
        s: [`Each tester gives one bit; you need enough bits to label every bottle.`,
          [`n testers give 2<sup>n</sup> outcomes; you need 2<sup>n</sup> ≥ 1000.`, `2<sup>9</sup> = 512 is too few; 2<sup>10</sup> = 1024 is enough.`, `With binary labels, the set of dead testers spells out the bottle number: <b>10</b> testers.`],
          `Nine testers can't work: 512 outcomes can't distinguish 1,000 bottles.`, `Testing bottles one by one, which needs far more people or rounds.`] }]],

    ['rabbit-123', 'Combinatorics', 'm', 'Hopping up the stairs',
      `A rabbit climbs a staircase hopping up 1, 2 or 3 steps at a time.`,
      [{ q: `In how many different ways can it climb 10 steps?`, a: 274, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Look at the last hop: it lands on step 10 from step 9, 8 or 7.`, `So the count for n steps is the sum of the counts for n − 1, n − 2 and n − 3.`, `Start with 1 way for 0 steps, 1 for 1 step and 2 for 2 steps, and build up.`],
        s: [`Condition on the last hop: f(n) = f(n − 1) + f(n − 2) + f(n − 3).`,
          [`f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1, f(2) = 2.`, `Then 4, 7, 13, 24, 44, 81, 149.`, `f(10) = 149 + 81 + 44 = <b>274</b>.`],
          `With only 1- and 2-step hops it's Fibonacci (89 ways for 10 steps); allowing 3s must give more.`, ``] }]],

    ['wood', 'Logic', 'e', 'Sharing the firewood',
      `Three friends share everything equally. A brings 5 logs, B brings 3 and C none. They burn all 8 logs cooking a shared meal, and C pays $8 for his share of the wood.`,
      [{ q: `How many of the $8 should go to A?`, a: 7, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Each person used a third of the 8 logs.`, `C pays for the wood he used; work out how much of that came from A and how much from B.`, `A used 8/3 of his own 5 logs, so he gave the rest to C.`],
        s: [`Pay each supplier for the wood that went to C, not in proportion to what they brought.`,
          [`Each used 8/3 logs. C's $8 buys 8/3 logs, so $3 a log.`, `A gave C 5 − 8/3 = 7/3 logs; B gave 3 − 8/3 = 1/3.`, `A gets 7/3 × 3 = <b>$7</b>, B gets $1.`],
          `7 + 1 = 8, and A supplied seven times as much of C's wood as B.`, `Splitting $5 : $3 in proportion to the logs brought.`] }]],

    ['hilbert', 'Logic', 'm', 'The infinite hotel',
      `A hotel has rooms 1, 2, 3, … without end, and every room is full.`,
      { open: `<p><b>One new guest:</b> move the guest in room n to room n + 1 for every n. Room 1 is free.</p><p><b>Infinitely many new guests (a countable queue):</b> move the guest in room n to room 2n. All the odd rooms are free; give the k-th newcomer room 2k − 1.</p><p><b>Infinitely many coaches, each with infinitely many passengers:</b> first move existing guests to the even rooms. Then give passenger p of coach c a room by a one-to-one rule from pairs (c, p) to odd numbers, for example room 3<sup>c</sup>·5<sup>p</sup> (distinct by unique factorisation, always odd), or by walking the (c, p) grid diagonal by diagonal. Pairs of whole numbers are countable, so everyone fits.</p>`,
        h: [`For one new guest, everyone must move without anyone leaving.`, `For infinitely many, free up an infinite set of rooms: what if each guest doubles their room number?`, `For infinitely many coaches you need a one-to-one map from pairs (coach, seat) to whole numbers. Think of primes, or of walking a grid diagonally.`] }],

    ['chuck-luck', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Chuck-a-luck',
      `You pay $1 and pick a number from 1 to 6. Three fair dice are rolled, and you're paid $1 for each die showing your number. Your $1 stake isn't returned.`,
      [{ q: `What is your expected payout?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`Use linearity: the payout is the sum of three indicators, one per die.`, `Each die shows your number with probability 1/6.`, `Alternatively, imagine six players covering all six numbers: how much is paid out in total on every roll?`],
        s: [`The payout is the number of dice showing your number; its expectation is a sum of three equal pieces.`,
          [`E = 3 × 1/6 = <b>$0.50</b>, so you lose 50 cents per dollar on average.`],
          `Six players covering every number are paid $3 in total on every roll, $0.50 each, even if the dice are biased.`, `Adding up the chances of "at least one match" and forgetting that a double pays twice.`] }]],

    ['languages', 'Combinatorics', 'h', 'Seventy people, many languages',
      `In a group of 70 people, for any two people X and Y there is a language X speaks that Y doesn't, and a language Y speaks that X doesn't.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest languages the group can speak in total?`, a: 8, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Each person corresponds to a set of languages, and no person's set can contain another's.`, `Such a family of sets is called an antichain. How large can an antichain of subsets of n languages be?`, `Sperner's theorem: the largest antichain of subsets of an n-set has C(n, ⌊n/2⌋) sets. Find the smallest n with that at least as large as the group.`],
        s: [`The language sets must form an antichain; Sperner's theorem gives the most people n languages can serve.`,
          [`With n languages, at most C(n, ⌊n/2⌋) people.`, `C(7, 3) = 35 is too few, but C(8, 4) = 70.`, `So <b>8</b> languages: give each person a different set of 4.`],
          `Each 4-language set has a language the other lacks, in both directions, since neither set contains the other.`, ``] }]],

    ['guess-toss', 'Probability', 'm', 'Guess the other coin',
      `A and B are in separate rooms. Each tosses a fair coin and then guesses the other's coin. The team wins $3 if both guesses are right, and it costs $1 to play. They can agree a strategy beforehand but can't communicate afterwards.`,
      [{ q: `(a) With the best strategy, what is the probability that both guess right?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`Guessing independently at random wins only a quarter of the time. Can their guesses be correlated?`, `Each player knows one thing: their own coin.`, `Try "guess that the other coin matches mine". When is each guess right?`],
        s: [`Make both guesses right on the same outcomes: each guesses the other's coin matches their own.`,
          [`If the coins match, both guesses are right; if not, both are wrong.`, `P(coins match) = <b>½</b>.`],
          `No strategy can beat ½: A's guess is right with probability ½ whatever she does, since B's coin is independent of everything A knows.`, `Answering ¼, as if the guesses had to be independent.`] },
      { q: `(b) What is the team's expected profit per game with that strategy, in dollars?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`Expected winnings are the prize times the probability of winning.`, `Then subtract the cost of playing.`],
        s: [`Expected profit = P(win) × prize − cost.`,
          [`½ × $3 − $1 = <b>$0.50</b>, so they should play.`],
          `With independent random guesses it would be ¼ × 3 − 1 = −$0.25.`, ``] }]],

    ['snowflakes', 'Markov chains', 'm', 'Snowflake patterns',
      `Snowflakes fall one after another. A flake is a stellar dendrite with probability 0.6 if the flake before it was one, and with probability 0.2 if it wasn't.`,
      [{ q: `In the long run, what fraction of flakes are stellar dendrites?`, a: 1 / 3,
        h: [`This is a two-state Markov chain; you want its stationary distribution.`, `If x is the long-run fraction, the next flake is a dendrite with probability 0.6x + 0.2(1 − x).`, `In the long run that must equal x. Solve.`],
        s: [`Stationary distribution of a two-state chain: x = px + q(1 − x), so x = q/(1 − p + q).`,
          [`x = 0.6x + 0.2(1 − x) ⇒ 0.6x = 0.2.`, `x = <b>1/3</b>.`],
          `Balance: flow into "dendrite" is 0.2 × 2/3 and flow out is 0.4 × 1/3, both 2/15.`, `Averaging 0.6 and 0.2 to get 0.4, ignoring how long the chain stays in each state.`] }]],

    ['kings-salary', 'Game theory', 'h', "The king's salary",
      `A city has 66 people, the king included, each paid $1. The king (who can't vote) proposes new whole-dollar salaries summing to $66. Each citizen votes yes if their pay goes up, no if it goes down, and abstains otherwise; a plan passes if yes votes outnumber no votes. The king can propose plan after plan.`,
      [{ q: `What is the most the king can end up paid?`, a: 63, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`A plan only needs a majority of the people whose pay changes, so the king can buy a bare majority of the paid citizens each round.`, `Concentrate all the money in fewer and fewer people: 65 voters, then 33, then 17, …`, `At the end, a handful of rich voters can be outvoted by citizens who now earn nothing and would welcome $1 each.`],
        s: [`Repeatedly raise a bare majority of the paid citizens and zero the rest, then outvote the last few rich ones with $1 bribes to paupers.`,
          [`Give 33 voters $2 and everyone else (the king too) $0: 33 yes, 32 no.`, `Then concentrate the $66 in 17 of those 33 (17 yes, 16 no), then 9, 5, 3 and 2 people.`, `Finally take the money from those 2, give $1 to each of 3 paupers and keep $63: 3 yes, 2 no. The king ends with <b>$63</b>.`],
          `Each step passes because the winners outnumber the losers by one.`, ``] }]],

    ['friends', 'Combinatorics', 'e', 'Counting friends',
      `At a party of n ≥ 2 people, some pairs are friends (friendship is mutual).`,
      { open: `<p>Each person has between 0 and n − 1 friends: n possible counts. But 0 and n − 1 can't both occur, since someone friends with everyone means no one has zero friends. So at most n − 1 different counts are used by n people, and by the <b>pigeonhole principle</b> two people have the same number of friends.</p>`,
        h: [`List the possible numbers of friends one person can have.`, `There are n of them, one for each of n people, unless two of those values can't happen together.`, `Can someone have 0 friends while someone else is friends with everyone?`] }],

    ['ratio', 'Probability', 'e', 'A random ratio',
      `p and q are independent uniform random numbers between 0 and 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that p/q lies between 1 and 2?`, a: 0.25,
        h: [`Picture (q, p) as a uniform point in the unit square.`, `p/q between 1 and 2 means q ≤ p ≤ 2q.`, `Find the area of that region inside the square. Watch where the line p = 2q leaves the square.`],
        s: [`Probability equals area in the unit square.`,
          [`The region q ≤ p ≤ 2q is the triangle between the lines p = q and p = 2q, cut off at p = 1.`, `Its vertices are (0, 0), (½, 1) and (1, 1): area ½ × ½ × 1 = <b>¼</b>.`],
          `By symmetry P(p/q > 1) = ½, and half of that lies between 1 and 2.`, ``] }]],

    ['monkey', 'Expected value', 'm', 'The banana-eating monkey',
      `A monkey rolls a fair die and eats that many bananas, except that on a 6 it eats 5 bananas and rolls again (and so on, as long as it keeps rolling 6s).`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of bananas eaten?`, a: 4, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Write E for the expected total and condition on the first roll.`, `On 1–5 the game ends; on a 6 the monkey eats 5 and the game starts afresh.`, `E = (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5)/6 + (1/6)(5 + E).`],
        s: [`Condition on the first roll: a 6 restarts the same game after 5 bananas.`,
          [`E = 15/6 + (5 + E)/6.`, `(5/6)E = 20/6, so E = <b>4</b>.`],
          `Each roll is a 6 with probability 1/6, so there are 6/5 rolls on average.`, ``] }]],

    ['heads-run-4', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Four heads in a row',
      `You toss a fair coin until you get four heads in a row.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of tosses?`, a: 30, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Let E<sub>n</sub> be the expected tosses to get n heads in a row.`, `Once you have n − 1 in a row, one more toss either finishes or sends you back to the start.`, `E<sub>n</sub> = E<sub>n−1</sub> + 1 + ½E<sub>n</sub>, which gives E<sub>n</sub> = 2E<sub>n−1</sub> + 2.`],
        s: [`Recursion: E<sub>n</sub> = 2E<sub>n−1</sub> + 2, so E<sub>n</sub> = 2<sup>n+1</sup> − 2.`,
          [`E<sub>1</sub> = 2, E<sub>2</sub> = 6, E<sub>3</sub> = 14.`, `E<sub>4</sub> = 2 × 14 + 2 = <b>30</b>.`],
          `2<sup>5</sup> − 2 = 30.`, `Answering 16, the waiting time you'd get if a failed run cost nothing.`] }]],

    ['chess-final', 'Probability', 'm', 'Meeting in the final',
      `A knockout chess tournament has 16 players of distinct strengths; the stronger player always wins. In every round the remaining players are paired at random.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that the two strongest players meet in the final?`, a: 8 / 15,
        h: [`The top two only lose to each other, so they meet in the final unless they're paired earlier.`, `In a round with m players, the strongest is paired with the second strongest with probability 1/(m − 1).`, `Multiply the chances of avoiding each other with 16, 8 and 4 players left.`],
        s: [`They reach the final together unless the random pairing matches them earlier.`,
          [`Avoid each other: 14/15 (16 left) × 6/7 (8 left) × 2/3 (4 left).`, `= 168/315 = <b>8/15 ≈ 0.533</b>.`],
          `In general N/(2(N − 1)), just over ½.`, ``] }]],

    ['hh-count', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Counting double heads',
      `A fair coin is tossed 10 times. Count the places where a head is immediately followed by a head (so HHH counts as 2).`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected count?`, a: 2.25,
        h: [`Use linearity of expectation over adjacent pairs of tosses.`, `How many adjacent pairs are there in 10 tosses?`, `Each pair is HH with probability ¼, whatever the others do.`],
        s: [`Linearity: the expected count is the number of adjacent pairs times P(HH).`,
          [`10 tosses have 9 adjacent pairs.`, `9 × ¼ = <b>2.25</b>.`],
          `For 2 tosses it gives ¼, and for 3 it gives ½, as expected.`, `Worrying that overlapping pairs aren't independent: linearity doesn't need independence.`] }]],

    ['stick-small', 'Probability', 'e', 'The smaller piece',
      `A stick of length 1 breaks at a uniformly random point.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected length of the shorter piece?`, a: 0.25,
        h: [`The shorter piece has length min(U, 1 − U).`, `By symmetry you can assume the break is in the left half.`, `Then the shorter piece is U, uniform on [0, ½].`],
        s: [`By symmetry, the shorter piece is uniform on [0, ½].`,
          [`E[min(U, 1 − U)] = ¼, the midpoint of 0 and ½.`, `So <b>0.25</b>, and the longer piece averages 0.75.`],
          `Shorter + longer = 1, and 0.25 + 0.75 = 1.`, ``] }]],

    ['rainbow-hats', 'Logic', 'h', 'Numbered hats',
      `N people each get a hat with a number from 0 to N − 1 (repeats allowed). Each sees everyone else's number but not their own, and all guess their own number at the same moment. They may agree a plan beforehand.`,
      { open: `<p>Number the people 0 to N − 1. Person i assumes the <b>total of all hats is congruent to i (mod N)</b> and guesses whatever number would make that true: (i − sum of the hats they see) mod N.</p><p>The real total is congruent to exactly one value m mod N, so person m's assumption is right and their guess is correct. Everyone else is wrong, but exactly one person is always right, which is all the plan needs.</p>`,
        h: [`Guesses are made at the same time, so no one can learn anything from the others. The plan must split the possibilities between people.`, `Consider the sum of all N hats, modulo N. It takes one of N values.`, `Assign each person one of those N values to assume.`] }],

    ['two-sums', 'Logic', 'm', 'Two questions, three unknowns',
      `I think of three positive whole numbers x, y and z. You may ask twice for the value of ax + by + cz with integers a, b, c of your choice (the second choice can depend on the first answer).`,
      { open: `<p>First ask for x + y + z (a = b = c = 1). Each number is at most that total, so it has at most d digits, where d is the number of digits of the total.</p><p>Then ask with (a, b, c) = (1, 10<sup>d</sup>, 10<sup>2d</sup>). The answer is x + y·10<sup>d</sup> + z·10<sup>2d</sup>: the last d digits are x, the next d are y and the rest are z, with no carrying because each is under 10<sup>d</sup>.</p>`,
        h: [`One number can carry a lot of information if the pieces don't overlap.`, `If you knew every number had at most d digits, what coefficients would let you read them all off one answer?`, `Use the first question to find a bound on the size of the numbers.`] }],

    ['domino', 'Logic', 'e', 'Dominoes on a clipped board',
      `Two opposite corners are cut from an 8 × 8 chessboard, leaving 62 squares.`,
      { open: `<p><b>It can't be covered by 31 dominoes.</b> Opposite corners are the same colour, so the board now has 32 squares of one colour and 30 of the other. Every domino covers one black and one white square, so 31 dominoes cover 31 of each. 30 ≠ 32, so no tiling exists.</p>`,
        h: [`Colour the board as a chessboard and count.`, `What colour are two opposite corners?`, `What does a single domino always cover?`] }],

    ['fruit-magic', 'Logic', 'm', 'The magic hat',
      `A hat holds 13 apples, 15 bananas and 17 cherries. When two different fruits collide they both turn into the third kind (an apple and a banana become two cherries, and so on).`,
      { open: `<p><b>No sequence of collisions leaves only one kind of fruit.</b> Look at the differences between the counts modulo 3. A collision changes two counts by −1 and the third by +2, so every difference between two counts changes by 0 or ±3: the differences mod 3 never change.</p><p>At the start, apples − bananas = −2, bananas − cherries = −2 and apples − cherries = −4: none is divisible by 3. If all 45 fruits were one kind, two of the counts would be 0 and their difference 0, which is divisible by 3. So that state can't be reached.</p>`,
        h: [`Look for something that doesn't change when two fruits collide (an invariant).`, `A collision changes the three counts by −1, −1 and +2. What does that do to the difference between two counts?`, `Work modulo 3.`] }],

    ['ants-card', 'Combinatorics', 'e', 'Catching ants',
      `51 ants sit on a 1 × 1 square table. You have a square card of side 1/5.`,
      [{ q: `How many ants can you guarantee to cover with the card, wherever the ants are?`, a: 3, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Split the table into squares the same size as the card.`, `How many small squares are there, and how many ants?`, `Some square must hold more than two ants (the generalised pigeonhole principle).`],
        s: [`Divide the table into 25 squares of side 1/5 and use the pigeonhole principle.`,
          [`51 ants in 25 squares: some square holds at least ⌈51/25⌉ = 3.`, `Place the card on that square: <b>3</b> ants.`],
          `With 50 ants you could have exactly 2 in every square, so 3 really needs the 51st ant.`, ``] }]],

    ['colour-grid', 'Combinatorics', 'h', 'A one-colour rectangle',
      `Every point with whole-number coordinates in the plane is coloured black or white.`,
      { open: `<p><b>Yes, there is always a rectangle with sides parallel to the axes and four corners of the same colour.</b> Look at the three rows y = 0, 1, 2. Each column contributes a pattern of three colours, and there are only 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 patterns. So among 9 columns two have the same pattern.</p><p>In that pattern three points are coloured with two colours, so two of them match. Those two rows, in those two columns, give four corners of the same colour.</p>`,
        h: [`Restrict to a few rows and look at each column as a pattern of colours.`, `With 3 rows, how many different column patterns are there?`, `Two identical columns, each containing two points of the same colour, make a rectangle.`] }],

    ['bulbs-ring', 'Logic', 'h', 'Light bulbs in a circle',
      `Bulbs 1 to n stand in a circle, all on. At step t you look at bulb t (mod n): if it's on, you toggle bulb t + 1; if it's off, you do nothing. You keep going round.`,
      { open: `<p>Call a state the pattern of bulbs together with your position. There are finitely many states, so some state repeats. Each step is <b>reversible</b>: from a state you can work out the one before it (go back one position; whether bulb t + 1 was toggled depends on bulb t, which this step didn't change). So the process is a permutation of a finite set, and every state lies on a cycle. The starting state (all on, position 1) therefore comes round again: <b>eventually all the bulbs are on again</b>.</p>`,
        h: [`The number of possible states (bulb pattern plus position) is finite.`, `Can you run the process backwards from any state, uniquely?`, `A reversible process on a finite set must return to where it started.`] }],

    ['chocolate', 'Logic', 'e', 'Breaking a chocolate bar',
      `A 6 × 8 chocolate bar is to be broken into its 48 squares. Each break splits one piece along a straight line; you can't stack pieces.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest breaks needed?`, a: 47, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Don't think about the shape. What does any single break do to the number of pieces?`, `You start with one piece and need 48.`],
        s: [`Every break increases the number of pieces by exactly one, whatever you break.`,
          [`From 1 piece to 48 pieces takes 48 − 1 = <b>47</b> breaks, and every order of breaking uses exactly that many.`],
          `An m × n bar always needs mn − 1 breaks.`, `Looking for a clever order that needs fewer: there isn't one.`] }]],

    ['zombies', 'Logic', 'm', 'Zombies on a line',
      `100 zombies stand a unit apart on a line, all walking at the same speed. The 50 on the left walk right and the 50 on the right walk left. When two meet, both turn round.`,
      [{ q: `How many collisions happen in total?`, a: 2500, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Two identical zombies bouncing off each other look exactly like two zombies passing through each other.`, `So count the meetings as if they walked straight through.`, `Every right-walker meets every left-walker that starts to its right.`],
        s: [`Pretend they pass through each other: the collisions are the crossings of right-walkers with left-walkers to their right.`,
          [`Each of the 50 right-walkers meets all 50 left-walkers.`, `50 × 50 = <b>2,500</b> collisions.`],
          `In general, add up, over each right-walker, the number of left-walkers to its right.`, `Tracking individual zombies as they bounce, which is hopeless.`] }]],

    ['infection', 'Logic', 'h', 'Infection on a board',
      `On an n × n board a square becomes infected when at least two of its four neighbours are infected.`,
      { open: `<p>Track the <b>perimeter</b> of the infected region (the number of edges between infected and healthy squares, including the board's edge). When a square is infected because at least two neighbours are, at least two of its edges stop being boundary and at most two new ones appear, so the perimeter never increases.</p><p>Fewer than n infected squares start with perimeter at most 4(n − 1) < 4n, but the whole board has perimeter 4n. So <b>fewer than n starting squares can never infect the whole board</b>.</p>`,
        h: [`Look for a quantity that never increases as the infection spreads.`, `Consider the total length of the boundary of the infected area.`, `Compare the starting boundary with the boundary of the full board.`] }],

    ['go-first', 'Probability', 'e', 'Who draws the red ball?',
      `A bag has 4 black balls and 1 red ball. Two players take turns drawing a ball without replacement, and whoever draws the red ball wins.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that the player who goes first wins?`, a: 0.6,
        h: [`Imagine all five balls are drawn regardless; the red ball's position in the order is uniformly random.`, `The first player draws positions 1, 3 and 5.`],
        s: [`The red ball is equally likely to be in any of the five draw positions.`,
          [`The first player gets positions 1, 3 and 5: <b>3/5</b>.`],
          `With an even number of balls in total, the two players would be level.`, `Answering ½ because "each player has the same chance on each draw".`] }]],

    ['postman', 'Expected value', 'h', 'The crazy postman',
      `A postman puts one letter in each of two empty boxes. Each further letter goes into a box chosen with probability proportional to the letters already in it. He delivers 10 letters in total.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of letters in the emptier box?`, a: 25 / 9,
        h: [`This is a Pólya urn. Its surprising fact: the count in the first box is uniformly distributed.`, `With 10 letters, the first box holds anything from 1 to 9 letters, each equally likely.`, `Average min(k, 10 − k) over k = 1, …, 9.`],
        s: [`In a Pólya urn starting from (1, 1), box 1's count is uniform on 1 to n − 1.`,
          [`k = 1, …, 9 equally likely; min(k, 10 − k) = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.`, `Sum 25, so the expectation is <b>25/9 ≈ 2.78</b>.`],
          `The fuller box then averages 10 − 25/9 ≈ 7.22: rich boxes get richer.`, `Expecting the boxes to stay roughly balanced.`] }]],

    ['single-bid', 'Expected value', 'm', 'A single bid',
      `A widget is worth V to its owner, uniform between $0 and $100 as far as you know. It's worth 1.8V to you. You make one offer x; the owner sells if x is more than V.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is your expected profit if you offer $50?`, a: -2.5,
        h: [`You win only when V is below your offer, so condition on that.`, `Given V < 50, V is uniform on 0 to 50, so its average is 25.`, `Expected profit = P(win) × (1.8 × E[V | win] − 50).`],
        s: [`Winning tells you V is low: you only buy widgets worth less than your bid.`,
          [`P(win) = 1/2 and E[V | win] = 25, worth 45 to you.`, `Expected profit = ½ × (45 − 50) = <b>−$2.50</b>.`],
          `For any offer x the expected profit is (x/100)(0.9x − x) &lt; 0.`, `Using the unconditional average V = 50, worth 90 to you.`] },
      { q: `(b) What offer maximises your expected profit?`, a: 0, tol: { abs: 0.5, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Write the expected profit as a function of x.`, `Winning means V < x, so V averages x/2 and is worth 0.9x to you.`, `Is (x/100) × (0.9x − x) ever positive?`],
        s: [`Adverse selection: whatever you offer, the widgets you win are worth less than you paid on average.`,
          [`Expected profit = (x/100)(0.9x − x) = −0.001x², which is negative for every x > 0.`, `So the best offer is <b>$0</b>: don't bid.`],
          `The 80% advantage would need to exceed 100% for any bid to pay.`, `Bidding just under the break-even of $55.`] }]],

    ['truck', 'Probability', 'm', 'Waiting for a truck',
      `Trucks pass a point as a Poisson process, on average one every 10 minutes. Your car breaks down there at a random moment.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is your expected wait, in minutes, for the next truck?`, a: 10, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Gaps between Poisson arrivals are exponential.`, `The exponential distribution is memoryless: how long it has already been doesn't matter.`],
        s: [`Memorylessness: the wait from any fixed moment is exponential with the same mean.`,
          [`Expected wait = mean gap = <b>10</b> minutes.`],
          `Running time backwards gives the same process, so the last truck also passed 10 minutes ago on average.`, `Answering 5, half the average gap.`] },
      { q: `(b) What is the expected length, in minutes, of the gap between trucks that you land in?`, a: 20, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Add the expected time since the last truck to the expected time until the next one.`, `The time back to the previous truck is, by symmetry, also exponential with mean 10.`, `This is the inspection paradox: a random moment is more likely to fall in a long gap.`],
        s: [`Inspection paradox: you land in gaps in proportion to their length.`,
          [`Backward wait 10 + forward wait 10 = <b>20</b> minutes, twice the average gap.`],
          `For exponential gaps, E[X²]/E[X] = 2 × 10 = 20, the length-biased mean.`, `Answering 10, the average gap.`] }]],

    ['archer', 'Probability', 'e', 'The blind archer',
      `A consistent archer fires three arrows; their distances from the centre are independent and identically distributed. You're told the first was closer than the second.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that the third is the closest of the three?`, a: 1 / 3,
        h: [`With identical, independent shots, all six orderings of the three distances are equally likely.`, `Keep only the orderings where shot 1 beats shot 2.`, `In how many of those is shot 3 best?`],
        s: [`Symmetry: all 3! orderings are equally likely; condition by keeping half of them.`,
          [`Orderings with 1 before 2: (1, 2, 3), (1, 3, 2), (3, 1, 2).`, `Shot 3 is best in one of the three: <b>1/3</b>.`],
          `The information about shots 1 and 2 says nothing about shot 3's rank against them.`, `Answering ½ by thinking it's now a race between shots 1 and 3.`] }]],

    ['min-max', 'Combinatorics', 'm', 'Minimum and maximum together',
      `You need both the smallest and largest of 100 numbers, using comparisons only.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest comparisons that always suffices?`, a: 148, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Doing the two searches separately costs 99 + 99.`, `Compare the numbers in pairs first: the smaller of each pair is a candidate for the minimum, the larger for the maximum.`, `That's 50 comparisons, then a min over 50 and a max over 50.`],
        s: [`Pair up first, so each element is a candidate for only one of the two searches.`,
          [`50 comparisons split the numbers into 50 winners and 50 losers.`, `Maximum of the winners: 49. Minimum of the losers: 49.`, `Total 50 + 49 + 49 = <b>148</b>, against 198 done separately.`],
          `In general ⌈3n/2⌉ − 2, which is known to be optimal.`, ``] }]],

    ['crazy-clock', 'Logic', 'm', 'The crazy clock',
      `Both hands of a clock start at 12. The minute hand goes clockwise round 5 times (at varying speeds) while the hour hand goes anticlockwise round 4 times, and both end back at 12 together.`,
      [{ q: `How many times do the hands pass each other, not counting the start and end?`, a: 8, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Look at the motion relative to the minute hand.`, `Relative to it, the hour hand goes round 5 + 4 times.`, `Each full relative lap ends in a meeting; drop the final one, which is at the end.`],
        s: [`In the minute hand's frame the hour hand makes 9 laps, meeting it once per lap.`,
          [`9 meetings including the one at the very end.`, `Excluding it: <b>8</b>.`],
          `Varying speeds don't matter: only the total relative angle counts.`, `Answering 1 (= 5 − 4), as if both turned the same way.`] }]],

    ['candy-circle', 'Logic', 'h', 'Passing candies round a circle',
      `Children sit in a circle, each with an even number of sweets. On each whistle every child passes half their sweets to the child on their left; then anyone with an odd number gets one more from the teacher.`,
      { open: `<p>Let M be the largest holding. After a whistle a child has half their own sweets plus half their neighbour's, so at most M, and topping up an odd number stays at most M because M is even. <b>The maximum never rises.</b></p><p>The minimum m never falls, and any child holding m whose right-hand neighbour holds more ends the round with more than m. So each round the run of children holding the minimum shrinks, until the minimum itself rises. The minimum can't rise past the maximum, and the teacher adds sweets each time, so this can't go on forever: eventually <b>everyone has the same number</b>.</p>`,
        h: [`Track the largest and smallest holdings.`, `Show the maximum can't increase and the minimum can't decrease.`, `Show the minimum (or the number of children holding it) must change for the better unless everyone is equal.`] }],

    ['coins-cover', 'Logic', 'h', 'Covering with overlapping coins',
      `100 coins of radius 1 lie on a rectangular table without overlapping, and no further coin can be added without overlapping.`,
      { open: `<p>Every point of the table is within distance 2 of some coin's centre: otherwise a radius-1 coin could be placed there without overlapping. So <b>coins of radius 2</b> on the same 100 centres cover the table.</p><p>Now shrink by a factor of 2: the table becomes four half-size copies arranged in a 2 × 2 grid. Cover each quarter with the halved arrangement of radius-1 coins: 4 × 100 = <b>400 coins of radius 1</b> cover the original table.</p>`,
        h: [`Why can't another coin be added? What does that say about the distance from any point to the nearest centre?`, `So doubling every coin's radius covers the table.`, `Turn "100 coins of radius 2" into "400 coins of radius 1" by scaling the table.`] }],

    ['scale-square', 'Logic', 'h', 'Growing a square of coins',
      `Four coins sit at the corners of a unit square. A move reflects one coin through another (the "jumper" lands on the far side of the "mirror", the same distance away).`,
      { open: `<p><b>You can't make a 2 × 2 square.</b> Every move keeps all coins on points with whole-number coordinates (a reflection of a lattice point through a lattice point is a lattice point). Moves are also reversible. So if a 1 × 1 square could grow into a 2 × 2 square, reversing the moves would shrink a 2 × 2 square into a 1 × 1 one, and repeating would shrink a 1 × 1 square into a ½ × ½ square, which needs points off the integer lattice. Contradiction.</p>`,
        h: [`Use coordinates: start the coins at (0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1) and (1, 1).`, `Where can a coin land after reflecting through another lattice point?`, `Moves are reversible. What would that let you do to a square if growing were possible?`] }],

    ['sequences', 'Sequences', 'm', 'Strange sequences',
      `Find the next term of each sequence.`,
      [{ q: `(a) 1, 11, 21, 1211, 111221, ?`, a: 312211, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Read each term aloud, describing the one before.`, `"1" is one 1, written 11; "11" is two 1s, written 21.`, `Describe 111221 in groups of equal digits.`],
        s: [`Look-and-say: each term describes the previous one digit by digit.`,
          [`111221 is three 1s, two 2s, one 1.`, `That reads <b>312211</b>.`],
          `Only digits 1, 2 and 3 ever appear in this sequence.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 20, 22, 101, ?`, a: 1010, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Each term is the same number written differently.`, `Try reading 11 in base 9 and 12 in base 8.`, `The next term is ten written in base 2.`],
        s: [`It's ten written in bases 10, 9, 8, … down.`,
          [`101 is ten in base 3.`, `Ten in base 2 is <b>1010</b>; the term after that would be ten 1s, in base 1.`],
          `Check: 22 in base 4 is 2 × 4 + 2 = 10.`, ``] }]],

    ['colour-runs', 'Expected value', 'h', 'Runs of colour',
      `A shuffled deck has 26 red and 26 black cards. A run is a maximal block of consecutive cards of the same colour.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of runs?`, a: 27, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`A new run starts at the first card and at every place where the colour changes.`, `Use linearity: runs = 1 + (number of colour changes between neighbours).`, `Given the first card of a pair, the next differs from it with probability 26/51: one card of the first colour is already used.`],
        s: [`Runs = 1 + number of adjacent pairs that differ; use linearity.`,
          [`Each of the 51 adjacent pairs differs with probability 26/51.`, `E = 1 + 51 × 26/51 = <b>27</b>.`],
          `For n red and n black cards it's n + 1.`, ``] }]],

    ['cube-return', 'Markov chains', 'm', 'Back to the start on a cube',
      `An ant walks along the edges of a cube, choosing each edge at random at every corner.`,
      [{ q: `Starting from a corner, what is the expected number of edges walked before it first returns to that corner?`, a: 8, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`For a random walk, the expected return time to a state is 1 divided by its long-run share of time.`, `On a regular graph the stationary distribution is uniform over the vertices.`, `A cube has 8 corners.`],
        s: [`Expected return time = 1/π(start), and the walk spends equal time at every corner.`,
          [`π = 1/8 for each corner.`, `Expected return time = <b>8</b> steps.`],
          `Getting to the opposite corner takes 10 steps on average, more than coming back, because the far corner is only reached from three neighbours.`, ``] }]],

    ['pole-distance', 'Probability', 'h', 'Distance from the North Pole',
      `A point is chosen uniformly on the surface of a sphere of radius 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is its expected straight-line distance (through the sphere) from the North Pole?`, a: 4 / 3,
        h: [`By Archimedes' hat-box theorem, the height of a uniform point on a sphere is uniform on [−1, 1].`, `If the point has height z, its squared distance to the pole (0, 0, 1) is 2 − 2z.`, `So average √(2 − 2z) over z uniform on [−1, 1].`],
        s: [`Height is uniform on [−1, 1], and the distance to the pole is √(2 − 2z).`,
          [`E = ½ ∫ from −1 to 1 of √(2 − 2z) dz. Put u = 2 − 2z, from 4 to 0: ¼ ∫ from 0 to 4 of √u du.`, `= ¼ × (2/3) × 4<sup>3/2</sup> = ¼ × 16/3 = <b>4/3</b>.`],
          `The distance runs from 0 to 2; 4/3 is above 1 because more of the sphere's area lies far from the pole.`, ``] }]],

    ['middle-piece', 'Probability', 'm', 'The middle piece',
      `A stick of length 1 is cut at two independent uniform points.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected length of the middle piece?`, a: 1 / 3,
        h: [`The middle piece has length |X − Y|.`, `Alternatively, by symmetry all three pieces have the same expected length.`, `The three lengths always add to 1.`],
        s: [`The three pieces are exchangeable, so each has mean one third.`,
          [`E[left] = E[middle] = E[right], and they add to 1.`, `E[middle] = <b>1/3</b>.`],
          `Direct check: E|X − Y| for two uniforms is 1/3.`, `Thinking the middle piece is shorter because "it's squeezed between two cuts".`] }]],

    ['envelope-matches', 'Expected value', 'e', 'Letters in the right envelopes',
      `n letters are put into n addressed envelopes at random, one per envelope.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of letters in their correct envelope?`, a: 1, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Write the count as a sum of indicators, one per letter.`, `Each letter lands in its own envelope with probability 1/n.`, `Linearity of expectation doesn't need the indicators to be independent.`],
        s: [`Linearity of expectation over n indicators, each with mean 1/n.`,
          [`E = n × 1/n = <b>1</b>, for every n.`],
          `For n = 2: both right or both wrong, each with probability ½, so E = 1.`, ``] }]],

    ['coupons', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Collecting coupons',
      `Each bottle of soda comes with one of 10 coupons, all equally likely. You win a prize when you have all 10.`,
      [{ q: `How many bottles do you expect to buy?`, a: 7381 / 252, tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Split the process into stages: each stage ends when you get a coupon you haven't seen.`, `With k coupons already, a new one turns up with probability (10 − k)/10 per bottle.`, `So that stage takes 10/(10 − k) bottles on average. Add them up.`],
        s: [`Sum of geometric waiting times: 10 × (1 + 1/2 + … + 1/10).`,
          [`E = 10/10 + 10/9 + … + 10/1 = 10 × H<sub>10</sub>.`, `H<sub>10</sub> ≈ 2.929, so E ≈ <b>29.29</b>.`],
          `In general n·H<sub>n</sub> ≈ n ln n + 0.58n; for n = 10 that's about 28.8.`, `Answering 10, or thinking the last few coupons are as quick as the first.`] }]],

    ['noodles', 'Expected value', 'h', 'Noodle loops',
      `A bowl holds a number of noodles. You repeatedly pick two free ends at random (every free end equally likely) and tie them together, until no free ends remain.`,
      [{ q: `(a) With 100 noodles, what is the expected number of loops?`, a: Array.from({ length: 100 }, (_, i) => 1 / (2 * i + 1)).reduce((s, x) => s + x, 0), tol: { abs: 0.01, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Each tie reduces the number of noodle pieces (strands or growing chains) by one, or closes a loop.`, `With k pieces left there are 2k free ends. Pick one end; the other end you tie it to is its own piece's other end with probability 1/(2k − 1).`, `Add those chances up as k goes from 100 down to 1.`],
        s: [`Each tie closes a loop with probability 1/(2k − 1) when k pieces remain; use linearity.`,
          [`E[loops] = 1/1 + 1/3 + 1/5 + … + 1/199.`, `That sum is about <b>3.28</b>.`],
          `It grows like ½ ln N, so even a million noodles give only about 8 loops.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) With 3 noodles, what is the probability of ending with a single loop through all of them?`, a: 8 / 15,
        h: [`For one big loop, no tie may close a loop until the very last one.`, `With k pieces left, a tie avoids closing a loop with probability (2k − 2)/(2k − 1).`, `Multiply for k = 3 and k = 2.`],
        s: [`Every tie except the last must join two different pieces.`,
          [`k = 3: 4/5. k = 2: 2/3.`, `P = 4/5 × 2/3 = <b>8/15</b>.`],
          `The last tie always closes the loop, so it isn't in the product.`, ``] }]],

    ['distinct-draws', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Distinct values drawn',
      `You draw 10 numbers, with replacement, uniformly from {1, …, 10}.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of distinct values drawn?`, a: 10 * (1 - 0.9 ** 10), tol: { abs: 0.005, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Use an indicator for each value: was it drawn at least once?`, `P(a given value is never drawn) = (9/10)<sup>10</sup>.`, `Add up 1 − (9/10)<sup>10</sup> over the ten values.`],
        s: [`Linearity over the ten values, each seen with probability 1 − (1 − 1/n)<sup>n</sup>.`,
          [`(0.9)<sup>10</sup> ≈ 0.349, so each value appears with probability ≈ 0.651.`, `E ≈ 10 × 0.651 = <b>6.51</b>.`],
          `For large n the fraction seen tends to 1 − 1/e ≈ 63%.`, ``] }]],

    ['first-face', 'Expected value', 'm', 'The first picture card',
      `You turn over a shuffled 52-card deck one card at a time.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected position of the first king or queen?`, a: 53 / 9,
        h: [`There are 8 kings and queens. The other 44 cards fall into the 9 gaps around them.`, `By symmetry each gap holds the same number of cards on average.`, `The first king or queen comes after the first gap.`],
        s: [`k special cards split the others into k + 1 equal-on-average gaps.`,
          [`44/9 non-special cards come before the first king or queen on average.`, `Position = 44/9 + 1 = <b>53/9 ≈ 5.89</b>.`],
          `For aces (4 special cards) the same argument gives 53/5.`, ``] }]],

    ['disc-point', 'Probability', 'm', 'A uniform point in a disc',
      `You turn two uniform random numbers X and Y on [0, 1] into a point in the unit disc: angle θ = 2πX and radius r = g(Y).`,
      [{ q: `For the point to be uniform over the disc, what must P(r ≤ ½) be?`, a: 0.25,
        h: [`Uniform over the disc means the probability of a region is proportional to its area.`, `The disc of radius ½ has a quarter of the area of the unit disc.`, `So the radius must satisfy P(r ≤ t) = t², which means r = √Y, not r = Y.`],
        s: [`P(r ≤ t) must equal the area fraction t², so take r = √Y.`,
          [`P(r ≤ ½) = (½)² = <b>¼</b>.`],
          `With r = Y, half the points would land inside radius ½, bunching them at the centre.`, `Using r = Y.`] }]],

    ['centre-inside', 'Probability', 'h', 'Enclosing the centre',
      `5 points are chosen independently and uniformly on a circle, and joined to make a pentagon.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that the centre of the circle lies inside the pentagon?`, a: 11 / 16,
        h: [`The polygon misses the centre exactly when all its vertices lie in some semicircle.`, `For n points, P(all in a semicircle) = n/2<sup>n−1</sup>: pick which point starts the semicircle clockwise.`, `Subtract that from 1.`],
        s: [`The centre is outside exactly when all points fit in a semicircle.`,
          [`P(all 5 in a semicircle) = 5/2<sup>4</sup> = 5/16.`, `P(centre inside) = 1 − 5/16 = <b>11/16</b>.`],
          `For a triangle it's 1 − 3/4 = 1/4, the classic answer.`, ``] }]],

    ['sum-past-one', 'Probability', 'm', 'Adding uniforms past 1',
      `You add up independent uniform random numbers on [0, 1] until the total exceeds 1.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that you need more than 3 numbers?`, a: 1 / 6,
        h: [`Needing more than 3 means the first 3 numbers add up to at most 1.`, `P(U₁ + … + U<sub>n</sub> ≤ 1) is the volume of a simplex.`, `That volume is 1/n!.`],
        s: [`More than n draws ⇔ the first n sum to at most 1, with probability 1/n!.`,
          [`P = 1/3! = <b>1/6</b>.`],
          `Summing P(N > n) over n gives E[N] = Σ 1/n! = e.`, ``] }]],

    ['walk-3-7', 'Random walks', 'm', 'Up 3 before down 7',
      `A symmetric random walk starts at 0 and moves +1 or −1 with equal probability each step.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is the probability it reaches +3 before −7?`, a: 0.7,
        h: [`The walk's position is a martingale: its expected value never changes.`, `When it stops it's at +3 with probability p and −7 otherwise, and the expected stopping position is 0.`, `Solve 3p − 7(1 − p) = 0.`],
        s: [`Optional stopping: E[final position] = 0.`,
          [`3p − 7(1 − p) = 0 ⇒ 10p = 7.`, `p = <b>0.7</b>.`],
          `In general b/(a + b): the nearer barrier is the likelier.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) What is the expected number of steps until it hits either barrier?`, a: 21, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Another martingale: X<sub>n</sub>² − n.`, `At the stopping time, E[X²] = E[number of steps].`, `E[X²] = 9 × P(+3) + 49 × P(−7).`],
        s: [`X<sub>n</sub>² − n is a martingale, so E[steps] = E[X²] at the stop.`,
          [`E[X²] = 9 × 0.7 + 49 × 0.3 = 6.3 + 14.7 = 21.`, `E[steps] = <b>21</b> (= 3 × 7).`],
          `The formula ab gives 3 × 7 = 21.`, ``] }]],

    ['candies-left', 'Probability', 'h', 'Candies left in the jar',
      `A jar holds 10 red, 20 blue and 30 green candies. You take them out one at a time at random.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that, at the moment the last red candy comes out, at least one blue and one green are still in the jar?`, a: 7 / 12,
        h: [`Think of the whole order of 60 candies. Look at which colour comes last overall.`, `If green is last, you also need a blue after the last red: among the blues and reds, the last must be blue.`, `Combine: P(green last) × P(blue after all reds) + P(blue last) × P(green after all reds).`],
        s: [`Condition on which colour is last overall; then compare the other two colours.`,
          [`Green last (30/60): need the last of the 30 red-and-blue candies to be blue: 20/30.`, `Blue last (20/60): need the last of the 40 red-and-green to be green: 30/40.`, `P = ½ × 2/3 + ⅓ × ¾ = 1/3 + 1/4 = <b>7/12</b>.`],
          `If red were last overall the event fails, and that has probability 1/6; 7/12 is well below 5/6.`, ``] }]],

    ['submarine', 'Game theory', 'h', 'Catching the submarine',
      `A plane sees an invisible submarine's position once. The submarine then moves in a straight line at constant speed v in an unknown direction. The plane flies at 2v and catches the submarine if it is ever directly above it.`,
      { open: `<p>After time t the submarine is somewhere on a circle of radius vt round its starting point. The plane first flies towards that point until it is on the expanding circle: starting distance d away, it meets the circle at distance d/3 from the point (it covers 2d/3 while the circle grows to d/3).</p><p>Then it <b>spirals outwards</b>, staying on the circle (its radial speed matches v, the rest of its speed 2v goes round). Within one full turn it passes over every direction, so it is above the submarine at some point. The spiral is logarithmic, r(θ) = (d/3)·e<sup>θ/√3</sup>, so it is caught by the time the circle's radius is (d/3)·e<sup>2π/√3</sup>.</p>`,
        h: [`Where can the submarine be after time t? Describe the set of possible positions.`, `If the plane can get onto that expanding circle, it can stay on it by moving outwards at speed v.`, `With its spare speed, the plane can sweep round the circle. One full turn covers every direction.`] }],

    ['spy-line', 'Logic', 'h', 'Catching the spy',
      `A spy starts at an unknown integer A on a line and moves B units each second (B an unknown integer, possibly negative). At each second you may ask "is the spy at position x?".`,
      { open: `<p>At time n the spy is at A + Bn. The pair (A, B) is one of countably many integer pairs, so list them all: (0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), (−1, 0), … walking outwards. At time n, take the n-th pair (a, b) on the list and ask whether the spy is at <b>a + bn</b>. When n reaches the position of the true (A, B) on the list, you ask exactly the right place. Every pair appears eventually, so the spy is caught in finite time.</p>`,
        h: [`At time n the spy is at A + Bn. You don't need to find A and B separately; just guess the pair.`, `How many possible pairs (A, B) are there? Can you list them all in one sequence?`, `At time n, test the position the n-th pair on your list would predict.`] }],

    ['gold-coins', 'Logic', 'h', 'Counting the gold coins',
      `You have N = 2<sup>k</sup> coins that look alike. Some are heavy gold, the rest light fakes. You have a two-pan balance and want to know how many are gold.`,
      { open: `<p>About <b>k(k + 1)/2 = O((log N)²) weighings</b> suffice. The key step: split any group of 2<sup>m</sup> coins into two halves whose number of gold coins differ by at most one, using O(m) weighings (for example, start with two halves, weigh them, and swap coins between them in a binary search until the pans balance or differ by one coin). Recurse on one half: knowing whether the halves balanced and the count in one half gives the count in the whole. That is T(N) = T(N/2) + O(log N), so O(log² N) in total.</p>`,
        h: [`One weighing tells you which group has more gold coins, not how many.`, `If you could split a group into two halves with (almost) equal numbers of gold coins, you'd only need to count one half.`, `How cheaply can you rebalance two halves using the balance?`] }],

    ['one-good-agent', 'Logic', 'h', 'Find one honest agent',
      `Among N agents fewer than half are traitors. You may ask agent i whether agent j is honest; honest agents always answer truthfully, traitors may say anything.`,
      { open: `<p>Use a chain (like finding a majority element). Keep a current candidate and a chain of people vouched for in sequence. Ask the last person in the chain about the next agent: if they say "honest", extend the chain; if "traitor", drop both of them (at least one is a traitor, so honest people remain a majority among the rest). When everyone has been processed, the last person in the surviving chain is honest: honest agents are a strict majority, so they can't all be cancelled, and anyone vouched for by an honest person is honest.</p><p>This uses at most N − 1 questions. If traitors were half or more, no strategy could work, since they could mimic the honest agents.</p>`,
        h: [`If A says B is a traitor, then at least one of A and B is a traitor. What happens if you discard both?`, `Discarding such pairs keeps the honest agents in the majority.`, `This is the same idea as the majority-vote (Boyer–Moore) algorithm.`] }],

    ['secret-locks', 'Combinatorics', 'h', 'Locks on the safe',
      `Five people want a safe that any 3 of them can open together but no 2 can. They fit several locks (all must be opened), each with keys that open only that lock.`,
      [{ q: `(a) What is the fewest locks needed?`, a: 10, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`For every pair of people, some lock must defeat them: neither holds its key.`, `That lock's keys go to the other 3 people, so any 3 including one of those can open it.`, `Can two different pairs share the same lock?`],
        s: [`Each pair needs its own lock whose keys are held by exactly the other three.`,
          [`Two pairs can't share a lock: together they involve at least 3 people, one of whom would need the key.`, `So one lock per pair: C(5, 2) = <b>10</b> locks.`],
          `Any 3 people can open every lock, because only the 2 people of that lock's pair lack its key.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) How many keys does each person carry?`, a: 6, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Each lock has 3 keys.`, `Count the total number of keys and share them out by symmetry.`],
        s: [`Count keys: 10 locks × 3 keys, shared equally by 5 people.`,
          [`30 keys / 5 = <b>6</b> each.`],
          `Each person lacks the keys to the C(4, 1) = 4 locks of the pairs including them: 10 − 4 = 6.`, ``] }]],

    ['weights', 'Combinatorics', 'm', 'Weighing with a balance',
      `You want to weigh every whole number of grams from 1 to 40 on a two-pan balance, putting weights on either pan.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest weights needed?`, a: 4, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Each weight can go on the left pan, the right pan, or neither: three choices.`, `Balanced ternary writes every number with digits −1, 0 and +1.`, `Weights 1, 3, 9, 27 reach every total up to 1 + 3 + 9 + 27.`],
        s: [`Balanced ternary: weights 1, 3, 9, 27 cover 1 to 40 exactly.`,
          [`With n weights there are at most (3<sup>n</sup> − 1)/2 positive totals.`, `n = 3 gives at most 13; n = 4 gives 40, achieved by 1, 3, 9, 27.`, `So <b>4</b> weights.`],
          `Check 2 = 3 − 1: weigh with the 3 on one pan and the 1 next to the object.`, `Answering 6 (powers of two: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32), which only uses one pan.`] }]],

    ['gas-stations', 'Logic', 'm', 'Petrol stations round a circuit',
      `Petrol stations round a circular road hold, between them, exactly enough fuel to drive once round.`,
      { open: `<p>Imagine driving round once starting anywhere, allowed to have negative fuel, and plot the tank level. Over the full lap the level returns to its starting value (total fuel = total distance). <b>Start at the station where this imaginary level is lowest.</b> From there the level never drops below its starting value of zero, so with a real empty tank you never run out.</p>`,
        h: [`Pretend the tank can go negative and track the fuel level over one lap from any starting point.`, `After a full lap the level is back where it started.`, `Where would starting make the whole graph sit at or above zero?`] }],

    ['bricks', 'Logic', 'h', 'Bricks in a box',
      `Can 53 bricks of size 1 × 1 × 4 be packed into a 6 × 6 × 6 box (faces parallel to the box)?`,
      { open: `<p><b>No.</b> Split the box into 27 cubes of size 2 × 2 × 2 and colour them in a 3D chessboard pattern: 14 of one colour and 13 of the other. Along any axis-parallel line the 2-cubes alternate colour, so a 1 × 1 × 4 brick always covers <b>2 unit cells of each colour</b> (2 + 2, or 1 + 2 + 1 across three cubes). The colour with only 13 cubes has 13 × 8 = 104 unit cells, so at most 104/2 = 52 bricks fit. 53 is impossible.</p>`,
        h: [`216 cells and 53 × 4 = 212, so volume alone doesn't rule it out. Look for a colouring.`, `Colour the box in 2 × 2 × 2 blocks, alternating like a 3D chessboard.`, `How many cells of each colour does any brick cover?`] }],

    ['eight-five', 'Logic', 'h', 'Positive eights, negative fives',
      `A finite sequence of real numbers has every 8 consecutive terms summing to a positive number and every 5 consecutive terms summing to a negative number.`,
      [{ q: `What is the longest such a sequence can be?`, a: 11, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Try to show 12 terms are impossible by adding the same terms up in two ways.`, `Write the 5-term windows as rows of a grid, each row shifted one place; then its columns are 8-term windows.`, `Rows sum negative, columns positive, but both add up the same grid.`],
        s: [`A double-counting grid shows 12 is impossible; 11 can be achieved.`,
          [`With 12 terms, arrange the eight 5-sums starting at positions 1–8 as rows; the columns are the five 8-sums starting at 1–5.`, `Total by rows is negative, by columns positive: contradiction.`, `So at most <b>11</b>, and 11 is possible (e.g. 1 at positions 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11 and −1.6 elsewhere).`],
          `In general the bound is a + b − 2 = 8 + 5 − 2 = 11.`, ``] }]],

    ['two-cylinders', 'Probability', 'h', 'Two crossing cylinders',
      `Two cylinders of radius 1 cross at right angles, their axes meeting.`,
      [{ q: `What is the volume of their intersection?`, a: 16 / 3,
        h: [`Slice perpendicular to both axes (along the third direction).`, `At height z each cylinder gives a strip of half-width √(1 − z²), so the slice is a square.`, `Integrate (2√(1 − z²))² from −1 to 1.`],
        s: [`Horizontal slices are squares of side 2√(1 − z²).`,
          [`V = ∫ from −1 to 1 of 4(1 − z²) dz = 4 × (2 − 2/3).`, `= <b>16/3</b>.`],
          `The inscribed unit sphere has slices that are circles inscribed in those squares, so V = (4/π) × (4π/3) = 16/3.`, ``] }]],

    ['colour-changes', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Colour changes',
      `An urn has 50 black and 50 white balls, drawn one at a time without replacement.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of times consecutive draws differ in colour?`, a: 50, tol: { abs: 0.001, rel: 0 },
        h: [`There are 99 adjacent pairs of draws.`, `Given the first ball of a pair, the next one is the other colour with probability (balls of the other colour) ÷ (balls left).`, `Use linearity.`],
        s: [`Linearity over the 99 adjacent pairs.`,
          [`E = 99 × 50/99 = <b>50</b>.`],
          `It's the expected number of runs minus 1 (51 − 1).`, ``] }]],

    ['divisor-game', 'Game theory', 'h', 'The divisor game',
      `The numbers 1 to N are written down. Players take turns choosing a remaining number and crossing out it and all its divisors. Whoever crosses out the last number wins.`,
      { open: `<p><b>The first player always has a winning strategy</b> (for N ≥ 2), by strategy stealing. The game is finite with no draws, so one player has a winning strategy. Suppose the second player did. Let the first player cross out just the number 1. If the second player's winning reply is some number x, the first player could instead have played x at the start: crossing out x also crosses out 1, giving exactly the same position. Then the first player can follow the second player's winning strategy. Contradiction, so the first player wins. (The argument doesn't say what the winning moves are.)</p>`,
        h: [`One of the two players must have a winning strategy. Which one?`, `Every move crosses out 1, since 1 divides everything.`, `If "cross out 1" could be answered by a winning move, could the first player have made that move instead?`] }],

    ['clan-size', 'Statistics', 'h', 'Estimating clan size',
      `A country is split into clans. 1,000 people chosen at random each report the size of their own clan. You want the average clan size.`,
      { open: `<p>The plain average of the reports is <b>biased upwards</b>: a clan of size n is n times as likely to be sampled as a clan of size 1 (size-biased sampling). Weight each report by 1/n to undo this. The estimate is the <b>harmonic mean</b> of the reported sizes:</p><p>average clan size ≈ (number of respondents) ÷ Σ (1/n<sub>i</sub>) over respondents.</p><p>Each respondent stands for 1/n of a clan, so Σ 1/n<sub>i</sub> estimates the number of clans sampled and 1,000 divided by it estimates people per clan.</p>`,
        h: [`Are big and small clans equally likely to be represented in a sample of people?`, `A clan with n members is n times as likely to be hit as a clan of one.`, `Reweight each answer by 1/n.`] }],

    ['infinite-hats', 'Logic', 'h', 'Infinitely many hats',
      `Countably many prisoners stand in a line, each seeing all the hats in front of them (red or blue) but none behind, and hearing nothing. Each must guess their own hat. They may agree a plan beforehand.`,
      { open: `<p>Call two infinite red/blue sequences equivalent if they differ in only finitely many places. Before the line-up, the prisoners use the axiom of choice to pick one representative sequence from each equivalence class.</p><p>Each prisoner sees all but finitely many hats, so they know which class the actual sequence is in, and therefore its agreed representative. Each guesses the colour that representative has in their own position. The actual sequence differs from the representative in only finitely many places, so <b>only finitely many prisoners guess wrong</b>.</p><p>(It's a non-constructive plan, which is why it needs the axiom of choice.)</p>`,
        h: [`Each prisoner sees all but finitely many of the hats.`, `Group infinite sequences that differ in only finitely many places.`, `If everyone agrees in advance on one representative per group, what should each prisoner guess?`] }],

    ['stop-roll-4', 'Optimal stopping', 'm', 'Up to four rolls',
      `You may roll a fair die up to four times, stopping whenever you like; you score the last roll.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected score with the best strategy?`, a: 89 / 18,
        h: [`Work backwards. With one roll left, the game is worth 3.5.`, `With two rolls left, keep a first roll only if it beats 3.5.`, `The values go 3.5, then 4.25, then 14/3; use 14/3 as the threshold for the first of four rolls.`],
        s: [`Backward induction: keep a roll if it beats the value of continuing.`,
          [`V<sub>1</sub> = 3.5, V<sub>2</sub> = 4.25, V<sub>3</sub> = 14/3 ≈ 4.67.`, `With four rolls, keep 5 or 6 (above 4.67): V<sub>4</sub> = (5 + 6)/6 + (4/6) × 14/3.`, `= 11/6 + 28/9 = <b>89/18 ≈ 4.94</b>.`],
          `Each extra roll adds less and less: 0.75, 0.42, 0.28.`, ``] }]],

    ['six-five', 'Expected value', 'm', 'Waiting for 6 then 5',
      `You roll a fair die repeatedly.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected number of rolls until a 6 is immediately followed by a 5?`, a: 36, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Let E be the expected rolls from scratch and F the expected rolls when the last roll was a 6.`, `From a 6: a 5 finishes, another 6 keeps you in the "just rolled 6" state, anything else restarts.`, `E = 1 + (1/6)F + (5/6)E and F = 1 + (1/6)×0 + (1/6)F + (4/6)E.`],
        s: [`Two-state recursion; a repeated 6 doesn't lose progress.`,
          [`From E: E = 6 + F. From F: (5/6)F = 1 + (2/3)E.`, `Substituting: (5/6)(E − 6) = 1 + (2/3)E ⇒ E/6 = 6.`, `E = <b>36</b>.`],
          `Patterns with no self-overlap, like 65, take 1/P(pattern) = 36. A pattern like 66 takes 42.`, ``] }]],

    ['nondecreasing', 'Combinatorics', 'h', 'Non-decreasing rolls',
      `A fair die is rolled 10 times.`,
      [{ q: `How many of the 6<sup>10</sup> possible sequences never decrease?`, a: 3003, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`A non-decreasing sequence is determined by how many times each face appears.`, `So count the ways to write 10 as an ordered sum of six non-negative counts.`, `Stars and bars: C(10 + 5, 5).`],
        s: [`Non-decreasing sequences ↔ face counts (n₁, …, n₆) adding to 10: stars and bars.`,
          [`C(15, 5) = <b>3,003</b>.`],
          `So the probability is 3003/6<sup>10</sup> ≈ 0.00005.`, ``] }]],

    ['same-pair', 'Probability', 'm', 'The same pair again',
      `Two dice are thrown, then thrown again.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability the second throw shows the same pair of faces as the first (in either order)?`, a: 11 / 216,
        h: [`Split by whether the first throw was a double.`, `A double (probability 1/6) is matched only by the same double: 1/36.`, `Two different faces (probability 5/6) can be matched two ways: 2/36.`],
        s: [`Condition on the first throw being a double or not.`,
          [`Double: 1/6 × 1/36 = 1/216.`, `Different faces: 5/6 × 2/36 = 10/216.`, `Total <b>11/216 ≈ 0.051</b>.`],
          `It's a little under 2/36: doubles are harder to repeat.`, `Answering 1/36 and forgetting the faces can come in either order.`] }]],

    // ---- variants of puzzles Theo already had in another form
    ['three-bullets', 'Probability', 'e', 'Three bullets in a row',
      `Three bullets sit in adjacent chambers of a six-chamber revolver. The cylinder is spun, the trigger pulled, and it clicks: empty. The gun is handed to you.`,
      [{ q: `(a) If the cylinder simply advances one chamber, what is your chance of a bullet?`, a: 1 / 3,
        h: [`Given the click, the hammer was on one of the 3 empty chambers, each equally likely.`, `Chambers advance in order. Which empty chamber is followed by a bullet?`],
        s: [`Condition on which empty chamber clicked; only one of them is followed by a bullet.`,
          [`Empty chambers E1, E2, E3 come before the block of bullets B1, B2, B3.`, `Only after E3 does a bullet follow: <b>1/3</b>.`],
          `With the bullets adjacent, the empties are adjacent too, which is what keeps this low.`, ``] },
      { q: `(b) If the cylinder is spun again first?`, a: 0.5,
        h: [`A fresh spin forgets the click.`, `3 of the 6 chambers hold bullets.`],
        s: [`A spin resets everything.`,
          [`3/6 = <b>½</b>, worse than not spinning.`],
          `Not spinning: 1/3. Spinning: 1/2. Don't spin.`, ``] }]],

    ['seven-pirates', 'Game theory', 'h', 'Seven pirates',
      `Seven pirates of strict seniority split 100 coins. The most senior proposes a split, everyone votes (the proposer too), and it passes with at least half the votes; otherwise the proposer is thrown overboard and the next one proposes. Pirates are rational, want coins most, and otherwise enjoy throwing people overboard.`,
      [{ q: `How many coins does the most senior pirate keep?`, a: 97, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Work up from 2 pirates, where the senior one takes everything with their own vote.`, `Each proposer needs half the votes, so buys the cheapest votes: pirates who would get nothing in the next round take 1 coin.`, `With 7 pirates the proposer needs 3 votes besides their own.`],
        s: [`Backward induction: buy the votes of the pirates who'd get nothing if you died.`,
          [`2 pirates: (100, 0). 3: (99, 0, 1). 4: (99, 0, 1, 0). 5: (98, 0, 1, 0, 1).`, `6: (98, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0). 7: pay 1 coin to each of the 3 pirates who get 0 in the 6-pirate plan.`, `The senior pirate keeps 100 − 3 = <b>97</b>.`],
          `The pattern is 100 − ⌊(n − 1)/2⌋ while there are enough coins.`, ``] }]],

    ['monty-four', 'Conditional probability', 'm', 'Monty Hall with four doors',
      `A car is behind one of four doors. You pick one. The host, who knows where the car is, opens one of the other doors that hides a goat. You then switch to one of the two remaining closed doors, chosen at random.`,
      [{ q: `What is your probability of winning the car?`, a: 3 / 8,
        h: [`Your first door has the car with probability 1/4, and the host can't change that.`, `So the two other closed doors share the remaining 3/4.`, `Switching at random picks one of those two.`],
        s: [`Your original door keeps its 1/4; the other two closed doors share 3/4 equally.`,
          [`Each other closed door has probability 3/8.`, `Switching to either one wins with probability <b>3/8</b>.`],
          `3/8 beats staying (1/4), as in the three-door game.`, `Thinking the three closed doors are now equally likely (1/3 each).`] }]],

    ['thousand-bulbs', 'Logic', 'e', 'A thousand bulbs',
      `1,000 bulbs are off. Person k toggles every bulb whose number is a multiple of k, for k = 1, 2, …, 1,000.`,
      [{ q: `How many bulbs are on at the end?`, a: 31, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Bulb n is toggled once for each divisor of n.`, `Divisors come in pairs, except when n is a perfect square.`, `Count the perfect squares up to 1,000.`],
        s: [`Only perfect squares have an odd number of divisors, so only they end on.`,
          [`31² = 961 ≤ 1000 < 1024 = 32².`, `So <b>31</b> bulbs are on.`],
          `With 100 bulbs the answer is 10, matching √100.`, ``] }]],

    ['von-neumann', 'Expected value', 'm', 'A fair toss from a biased coin',
      `A coin lands heads with probability 0.8. To get a fair result you toss it in pairs: HT means "you", TH means "me", and HH or TT means toss another pair.`,
      [{ q: `What is the expected total number of tosses?`, a: 6.25,
        h: [`Each pair succeeds (HT or TH) with probability 2p(1 − p).`, `The number of pairs is geometric.`, `Expected tosses = 2 × expected pairs.`],
        s: [`Pairs until success are geometric with success probability 2p(1 − p).`,
          [`2 × 0.8 × 0.2 = 0.32, so 1/0.32 = 3.125 pairs on average.`, `Tosses = 2 × 3.125 = <b>6.25</b>.`],
          `The formula 1/(p(1 − p)) gives 1/0.16 = 6.25.`, ``] }]],

    ['three-eggs', 'Combinatorics', 'h', 'Three eggs, 100 floors',
      `You have three identical eggs and a 100-floor building. An egg breaks when dropped from floor f or higher and survives below that. You want to find f for certain.`,
      [{ q: `What is the fewest drops that guarantees finding f?`, a: 9, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`Flip the question: with d drops and e eggs, how many floors can you handle?`, `F(d, e) = F(d − 1, e − 1) + F(d − 1, e) + 1, since the first drop either breaks or doesn't.`, `For 3 eggs this works out to C(d, 1) + C(d, 2) + C(d, 3). Find the smallest d with that at least 100.`],
        s: [`With d drops and 3 eggs you can cover C(d, 1) + C(d, 2) + C(d, 3) floors.`,
          [`d = 8: 8 + 28 + 56 = 92, too few.`, `d = 9: 9 + 36 + 84 = 129 ≥ 100.`, `So <b>9</b> drops.`],
          `Two eggs need 14 drops; the third egg saves 5.`, ``] }]],

    ['tuesday-boy', 'Conditional probability', 'h', 'A boy born on a Tuesday',
      `A family has two children. You learn that at least one of them is a boy born on a Tuesday. Each child is a boy or girl with probability ½ and born on any weekday with probability 1/7, independently.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that both are boys?`, a: 13 / 27,
        h: [`List child types as (sex, day): 14 equally likely types per child, 196 pairs.`, `Count pairs with at least one Tuesday boy: 14 + 14 − 1.`, `Count those where both are boys: 7 + 7 − 1.`],
        s: [`Count equally likely (sex, day) pairs.`,
          [`Pairs with at least one Tuesday boy: 14 + 14 − 1 = 27.`, `Of those, both boys: 7 + 7 − 1 = 13.`, `P = <b>13/27 ≈ 0.48</b>.`],
          `The extra detail moves the answer from 1/3 towards 1/2: the more specific the fact, the closer it gets to identifying one child.`, `Saying the weekday is irrelevant and answering 1/3.`] }]],

    ['witches-30', 'Probability', 'e', 'Two witches at the café',
      `Two witches each arrive at a café at an independent uniform time within the same hour, and each stays exactly 30 minutes.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability they are in the café at the same time?`, a: 0.75,
        h: [`Plot the two arrival times as a point in a unit square (in hours).`, `They overlap when the arrival times differ by at most ½.`, `The miss region is two corner triangles; find their area.`],
        s: [`Area in the unit square where |x − y| ≤ ½.`,
          [`Missing regions: two triangles with legs ½, total area 2 × ½ × ¼ = ¼.`, `P(meet) = 1 − ¼ = <b>¾</b>.`],
          `With stays of w hours it's 1 − (1 − w)².`, ``] }]],

    ['seat-99', 'Probability', 'h', 'The second-to-last passenger',
      `100 passengers board a plane in seat order. The first sits in a random seat; everyone else takes their own seat if it's free and a random free seat if not.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability that passenger 99 gets their own seat?`, a: 2 / 3,
        h: [`Whenever someone has to choose at random, seat 1 and the seats of everyone still to board are equally likely to be taken.`, `By the time passenger 99 boards, consider only seats 1, 99 and 100.`, `Passenger 99 is displaced only if seat 99 is taken before seats 1 and 100 are.`],
        s: [`Among the "critical" seats (1, 99, 100), each random choice is symmetric.`,
          [`The first of seats 1, 99, 100 to be taken is equally likely to be any of them.`, `If it's 99, passenger 99 is displaced; otherwise they sit in their own seat.`, `P = <b>2/3</b>.`],
          `For the last passenger the same argument gives 1/2.`, ``] }]],

    ['break-longer', 'Probability', 'h', 'Break the longer piece',
      `A stick of length 1 is broken at a uniform random point. Then the longer piece is broken at a uniform random point along its length.`,
      [{ q: `What is the probability the three pieces form a triangle?`, a: 2 * Math.log(2) - 1, tol: { abs: 0.002, rel: 0 },
        h: [`Every piece must be shorter than ½.`, `Say the first break leaves a longer piece of length L (uniform on [½, 1]). The second break at uniform position in it must leave both parts under ½.`, `Given L, that happens with probability (1 − L)/L. Average over L.`],
        s: [`Condition on the longer piece's length L, uniform on [½, 1].`,
          [`Given L, the cut must land within ½ of both ends: a window of length 1 − L out of L.`, `P = 2 ∫ from ½ to 1 of (1 − L)/L dL = 2(ln 2 − ½).`, `= 2 ln 2 − 1 ≈ <b>0.386</b>.`],
          `Breaking the longer piece helps: two random cuts on the whole stick give only 0.25.`, ``] }]],

    ['ten-colour-hats', 'Logic', 'h', 'Hats in ten colours',
      `100 prisoners in a line each wear a hat in one of 10 colours. Each sees all the hats in front, none behind. From the back, each names a colour aloud, and goes free if it matches their own. Everyone hears every answer.`,
      [{ q: `How many can a plan guarantee to free?`, a: 99, tol: EXACT,
        h: [`With two colours, the back prisoner announces a parity. Generalise.`, `Number the colours 0 to 9.`, `Let the back prisoner announce the sum of the hats they see, mod 10.`],
        s: [`The back prisoner announces the total of the other hats mod 10; everyone else deduces their own.`,
          [`Each later prisoner subtracts the hats they see and the colours already called (all correct) from the announced total, mod 10.`, `What's left is their own colour: <b>99</b> guaranteed.`],
          `It works for any number of colours: use the sum modulo the number of colours.`, ``] }]],
  ]);
})();
